-- title:
--   j(qᵖ) lies in ℚ(j(q),j(q^{p^2}))
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number carrying the hypothesis that $p$ is prime. Inside the field of Laurent series $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ \mathbb{Q}$ (Hahn series over $\mathbb{Q}$ with integer exponents), write $jq$ for the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, and let $qExpand\ \mathbb{Q}\ N$ be the ring endomorphism that multiplies all exponents by $N$, i.e. the substitution $q \mapsto q^{N}$. The element $jqN\ p = qExpand\ \mathbb{Q}\ p\ jq$, that is $j(q^{p})$, is asserted to be a member of $modularFunctionField\ (p\cdot p)$, which by definition is the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}\bigl(jq,\ qExpand\ \mathbb{Q}\ (p\cdot p)\ jq\bigr)$ obtained by adjoining to $\mathbb{Q}$ the two Laurent series $j(q)$ and $j(q^{p^{2}})$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ \mathbb{Q}$. Thus $j(q^{p})$ is a rational-coefficient algebraic expression in $j(q)$ and $j(q^{p^{2}})$ alone.
--
--   This is the intermediate step in the determination of the field generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^{N})$: $j(q^{p})$ is a common root of $\Phi_p(j(q), Y)$ and, using the symmetry of the prime-level modular polynomial, of $\Phi_p(j(q^{p^{2}}), Y)$, and uniqueness of that common root forces it into the base field. It is used by [`ModularCurve.functionFieldGeneration_sq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.functionFieldGeneration_sq).
